        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best espresso machine for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most beginners, a semi-automatic machine with a built-in grinder, such as the Breville Barista Express, is the easiest way to learn real espresso without buying a separate grinder. If you mostly drink lattes and already own a grinder, the Bambino Plus's automatic milk frothing makes consistent drinks from day one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 15-bar espresso machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Pump ratings are maximums. Espresso is typically brewed at around 9 bar at the puck, which is why the Gaggia Classic Evo Pro's updated spring limits brewing to 9 bar. A higher pump number does not mean better espresso; temperature control and grind quality matter more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dual boiler worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dual boiler is worth it if you regularly make several milk drinks in a row, because you can brew and steam at the same time with stable temperature. For one or two drinks a day, a fast-heating single-boiler or ThermoJet machine is more practical and far cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Super-automatic or semi-automatic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a super-automatic if convenience matters most and you are happy with a lighter, less concentrated shot. Choose a semi-automatic if you want the best flavour for the money and enjoy a hands-on routine. Many households with mixed preferences are happiest with a super-automatic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do home espresso machines last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lifespan depends heavily on water quality and maintenance. Regular descaling, backflushing where supported, and replacing gaskets as they wear help machines last many years. Machines with widely available parts, like the Gaggia Classic, are easier to keep running.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use pre-ground coffee in an espresso machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but results suffer because pre-ground coffee goes stale quickly and cannot be adjusted to the machine. Pressurised baskets, like those on budget machines, are more forgiving of pre-ground coffee. For good espresso, grind fresh.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What water should I use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use filtered water that is not too hard and not completely demineralised. Filtered tap water reduces scale and improves flavour. Avoid fully distilled water in most machines, as some minerals help extraction and certain machines rely on them for sensors. Check your manual.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

